export const databaseOptimizationIndexing = "## Introduction\n\n**A slow report is almost never a hardware problem — it is usually a missing or misused index.**\n\nI once inherited an analytics dashboard where the \"revenue by month\" report took 42 seconds to render. The database had 18 million rows in `orders`, decent hardware, and no obvious lock contention. The fix was not a bigger box — it was three well-chosen indexes and a rewrite of one paginated query. The report now returns in under 80 milliseconds.\n\nThis article is the practical playbook I use in PostgreSQL: how to read `EXPLAIN ANALYZE`, how to design B-tree and composite indexes, when covering and partial indexes pay off, how to index text and JSONB, and — just as important — when *not* to add an index. Every SQL block below is real and runnable against a typical `orders` / `users` schema.\n\n## Reading EXPLAIN ANALYZE\n\n`EXPLAIN` shows the planner's *estimated* plan; `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` actually *runs* the query and reports what happened. Always add `BUFFERS` — it tells you how much data was read from cache versus disk.\n\n```sql\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42;\n```\n\nA problematic output looks like this:\n\n```\nSeq Scan on orders  (cost=0.00..389211.00 rows=1 width=64)\n                    (actual time=0.412..812.006 rows=37 loops=1)\n  Filter: (customer_id = 42)\n  Rows Removed by Filter: 17999963\n  Buffers: shared read=209812\nPlanning Time: 0.098 ms\nExecution Time: 812.145 ms\n```\n\nThree signals matter here:\n\n- **Seq Scan on a big table** — PostgreSQL read all 18M rows to find 37. That is the smell.\n- **`Rows Removed by Filter`** — 18M rows discarded is pure wasted I/O.\n- **Estimated vs actual rows** — plan says `rows=1`, reality is `rows=37`. Small skews are fine; large ones (orders of magnitude) mean the planner's statistics are stale and it may be picking a bad plan.\n\nAfter adding an index on `customer_id`, the same query becomes an `Index Scan` reading a handful of buffers instead of 200k.\n\n<div class=\"callout c-perf\"><span class=\"ic\">🚀</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Performance tip</b><p>Read plans from the <b>innermost/most-indented</b> node outward — that is where execution starts. The <code>actual time</code> on a node is cumulative and multiplied by <code>loops</code>, so a node showing <code>0.5ms</code> with <code>loops=10000</code> is really 5 seconds.</p></div></div>\n\n## B-tree indexes and column order\n\nThe default `CREATE INDEX` builds a B-tree, which is the right choice for equality (`=`), ranges (`<`, `>`, `BETWEEN`), sorting, and `IN` lists. The subtle part is **composite index column order**.\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer_created\n    ON orders (customer_id, created_at);\n```\n\nThe rule of thumb: **equality columns first, then the range/sort column last.** A composite index is only usable as a prefix. The index above serves all of these:\n\n- `WHERE customer_id = 42`\n- `WHERE customer_id = 42 AND created_at >= '2026-01-01'`\n- `WHERE customer_id = 42 ORDER BY created_at`\n\nBut it does **not** help `WHERE created_at >= '2026-01-01'` alone, because `created_at` is not the leading column.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[Slow query] --> B[Run EXPLAIN ANALYZE]\n    B --> C{Seq scan on big table?}\n    C -- Yes --> D[Add matching index]\n    C -- No --> E[Check join and sort costs]\n    D --> F{Filter equality plus range?}\n    F -- Yes --> G[Composite: equality then range]\n    F -- No --> H[Single column index]\n    G --> I[Re-measure]\n    H --> I\n    E --> I\n```\n\n<div class=\"callout c-warn\"><span class=\"ic\">⚠️</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Common mistake</b><p>Putting the range column first, e.g. <code>(created_at, customer_id)</code>. Once the index scans a range on the leading column it can no longer use later columns for efficient seeking — it degrades to filtering. Lead with the columns you compare by equality.</p></div></div>\n\n## Covering indexes and index-only scans\n\nIf an index contains *every column a query needs*, PostgreSQL can answer entirely from the index without touching the table heap — an **Index Only Scan**. Use `INCLUDE` to add non-key payload columns without bloating the searchable key.\n\n```sql\n-- Query: SELECT status, total FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42;\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer_covering\n    ON orders (customer_id) INCLUDE (status, total);\n```\n\n`INCLUDE` columns are stored in the leaf pages but not used for ordering or range predicates. This keeps the key narrow (better for lookups) while still enabling index-only scans.\n\n<div class=\"callout c-tip\"><span class=\"ic\">💡</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Pro tip</b><p>Index-only scans still consult the visibility map. If a table has many recently-updated rows, the plan may show <code>Heap Fetches</code> greater than zero, partially defeating the optimization. Run <code>VACUUM</code> to keep the visibility map current so index-only scans stay \"only\".</p></div></div>\n\n## Partial indexes\n\nA **partial index** covers only rows matching a `WHERE` clause. It is smaller, faster to maintain, and ideal when queries always target a subset — for example, active orders in a table where most rows are archived.\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_pending\n    ON orders (created_at)\n    WHERE status = 'pending';\n```\n\nIf 95% of a 20M-row table is `completed`, this index might hold only 1M entries. Queries with a matching predicate (`WHERE status = 'pending' ORDER BY created_at`) use it; everything else ignores it entirely, so writes to completed rows never touch it.\n\nPartial indexes are also the clean way to enforce conditional uniqueness:\n\n```sql\n-- Only one active subscription per user; cancelled ones don't count\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX uniq_active_subscription\n    ON subscriptions (user_id)\n    WHERE status = 'active';\n```\n\n## Indexing text and JSONB\n\nB-trees can accelerate prefix matches (`LIKE 'abc%'`) with the right operator class, but they cannot help `LIKE '%abc%'`. For substring and fuzzy search, use a **GIN index with trigrams**.\n\n```sql\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_trgm;\n\nCREATE INDEX idx_users_name_trgm\n    ON users USING gin (full_name gin_trgm_ops);\n\n-- Now this uses the index instead of a seq scan:\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE full_name ILIKE '%prasad%';\n```\n\nFor **JSONB** containment and key-existence queries, GIN is again the tool:\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_events_payload\n    ON events USING gin (payload jsonb_path_ops);\n\n-- Matched by the index via the @> containment operator:\nSELECT * FROM events WHERE payload @> '{\"type\": \"signup\"}';\n```\n\n`jsonb_path_ops` produces a smaller index that supports `@>` containment; the default `jsonb_ops` is larger but also supports key-existence operators like `?`.\n\n| Index type | Best for | Example predicate |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| B-tree | equality, ranges, sorting | `WHERE id = 5`, `ORDER BY created_at` |\n| B-tree + prefix opclass | left-anchored text | `WHERE email LIKE 'a%'` |\n| GIN + `pg_trgm` | substring / fuzzy text | `WHERE name ILIKE '%foo%'` |\n| GIN (jsonb) | JSONB containment/keys | `WHERE data @> '{...}'` |\n| GiST / BRIN | ranges, huge append-only | geometry, time-ordered logs |\n\n## When NOT to index\n\nEvery index is a write tax. On each `INSERT`, `UPDATE` (of an indexed column), or `DELETE`, PostgreSQL must maintain every relevant index. A table with ten indexes can be an order of magnitude slower to write than the same table with two.\n\nSkip an index when:\n\n- **Low selectivity.** A boolean or a status column where 60% of rows share one value will be ignored by the planner anyway — a seq scan is cheaper than reading a huge fraction of the index plus random heap fetches. (A *partial* index on the rare value is the exception.)\n- **Small tables.** Under a few thousand rows, a seq scan is often faster than an index lookup.\n- **Write-heavy, rarely-queried columns.** You pay the maintenance cost on every write for a lookup that almost never happens.\n- **Redundant prefixes.** If you have `(a, b)`, a separate index on `(a)` is usually wasted — the composite already serves `a`-only lookups.\n\n<div class=\"callout c-int\"><span class=\"ic\">🔥</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Interview question</b><p>\"Why did adding an index make my query <i>slower</i>?\" Likely answers: the column has low selectivity so the index scan does more random I/O than a seq scan; or planner statistics are stale (<code>ANALYZE</code> the table); or the extra index slowed the write path being measured. Use <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> to prove which.</p></div></div>\n\n## Keyset pagination and avoiding N+1\n\nDeep `OFFSET` pagination is a classic slow-report cause. `OFFSET 100000 LIMIT 20` forces PostgreSQL to fetch and discard 100,000 rows before returning 20 — cost grows linearly with page depth.\n\n```sql\n-- Slow on deep pages: reads and throws away 100000 rows\nSELECT id, total, created_at\nFROM orders\nORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC\nOFFSET 100000 LIMIT 20;\n```\n\n**Keyset (cursor) pagination** instead seeks directly using the last row seen, staying constant-time on any page:\n\n```sql\n-- Fast on any page: seeks with the index, reads only 20 rows\nSELECT id, total, created_at\nFROM orders\nWHERE (created_at, id) < ('2026-06-01 10:00:00', 918273)\nORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nThe row-value comparison `(created_at, id) < (...)` maps perfectly onto an index on `(created_at DESC, id DESC)`, so the database jumps straight to the cursor position.\n\nThe other silent report killer is the **N+1 query**: fetching a list of 100 orders, then firing one query per order to load its customer. That is 101 round trips. Collapse it into a single join or an `IN` batch:\n\n```sql\n-- One query instead of 1 + N\nSELECT o.id, o.total, c.name\nFROM orders o\nJOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.created_at >= '2026-06-01';\n```\n\n<div class=\"callout c-tip\"><span class=\"ic\">💡</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Pro tip</b><p>Most ORMs cause N+1 by lazily loading associations in a loop. Reach for eager loading (<code>JOIN</code> / <code>includes</code> / <code>selectinload</code>) and confirm with query logging that a page issues a bounded number of statements, not one per row.</p></div></div>\n\n## VACUUM, ANALYZE, and statistics\n\nThe planner's decisions are only as good as its statistics. `ANALYZE` samples a table and updates the distribution stats (row counts, most-common values, histograms) that drive estimates. When estimated rows diverge wildly from actual rows, run `ANALYZE` first — before adding indexes.\n\n```sql\nANALYZE orders;\n\n-- For a column with skewed data, raise the sample resolution:\nALTER TABLE orders ALTER COLUMN status SET STATISTICS 1000;\nANALYZE orders;\n```\n\n`VACUUM` reclaims space from dead tuples (left by updates and deletes) and refreshes the visibility map that index-only scans depend on. Autovacuum handles this automatically, but on high-churn tables you may need to tune its thresholds so it runs before bloat degrades plans.\n\nFor a big data-loading job or a table that just changed shape dramatically, a manual `VACUUM (ANALYZE) orders;` gives the planner a fresh, accurate picture immediately rather than waiting for autovacuum.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n<div class=\"takeaways\"><ul>\n<li>Start every investigation with <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code>: watch for seq scans on big tables, high <code>Rows Removed by Filter</code>, and estimate-vs-actual skew.</li>\n<li>In composite B-tree indexes, order columns as equality first, range/sort last — the index is only usable as a left prefix.</li>\n<li>Use <code>INCLUDE</code> covering indexes to get index-only scans, and partial indexes to shrink indexes and enforce conditional uniqueness.</li>\n<li>Use GIN + <code>pg_trgm</code> for substring/fuzzy text and GIN for JSONB containment — B-trees cannot do <code>LIKE '%x%'</code>.</li>\n<li>Don't index low-selectivity columns or tiny tables; every index is a tax on every write.</li>\n<li>Replace deep <code>OFFSET</code> with keyset pagination, kill N+1 with joins, and keep <code>ANALYZE</code>/<code>VACUUM</code> current so plans stay accurate.</li>\n</ul></div>\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>How do I find which queries need indexes?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>Enable the <code>pg_stat_statements</code> extension to rank queries by total and mean execution time, then run <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> on the worst offenders. Look for sequential scans on large tables and large gaps between estimated and actual rows.</p></div></details>\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>Should I use CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY in production?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>Yes. A plain <code>CREATE INDEX</code> takes a lock that blocks writes for the duration. <code>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code> builds the index without blocking writes, at the cost of being slower and unable to run inside a transaction block. It is the safe default on live tables.</p></div></details>\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>Why is my index being ignored by the planner?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>Common causes: the predicate is not sargable (e.g. <code>WHERE lower(email) = ...</code> without a matching expression index), the column has low selectivity so a seq scan is genuinely cheaper, statistics are stale (<code>ANALYZE</code> the table), or a type mismatch forces an implicit cast. Check the plan and compare estimated rows to reality.</p></div></details>\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>Do more indexes always make reads faster?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>No. Redundant or overlapping indexes add write overhead and give the planner more (sometimes worse) options without improving the queries you actually run. Index for real query patterns, then periodically drop unused indexes — <code>pg_stat_user_indexes</code> shows which ones have zero scans.</p></div></details>\n";
